00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:07.450
OK, now, coming
back to what we're

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:14.440
talking in the last lecture,
we were discussing really the--

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:19.630
now, chapter 8, the title
is the Coupled Transport

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.250
and Energy Conversion.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:27.130
And my view here is, when
we think about the energy

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.400
conversion, it's
microscopically,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.370
it's a one form of
the energy carrier

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.510
transform the energy
into the other form.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:45.100
And that coupling process what,
when we use Boltzmann equation,

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.510
is reflected in the
scattering term.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:53.650
In the scattering term, we
have a scattering rate, which

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.280
approximately, in the
perturbation limit,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:02.170
we can use the Fermi Golden Rule
to calculate that scattering

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:02.950
rate.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:06.830
So we took electron phonon
coupling as an example.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:11.350
We say, OK, if I have the
phonon lattice vibrate

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:14.980
and change the separation
between the atoms,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.800
and that's effectively
changing the periodicity

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:19.810
of that electron
experience, and that's

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.930
the deformation potential.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:23.830
And with that
deformation potential,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:29.720
we actually went through
the derivation right

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.740
down the Fermi
Golden Rule and say,

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:36.020
what's that scatter
matrix look like?

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.020
And from that, we
say that actually

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.030
gives the [INAUDIBLE]
Fermi Golden Rule

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.470
and the manipulation
of scattering matrix

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:47.890
naturally give you the energy
and the momentum conservation.

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:53.230
And then we moved on to say,
OK, if I plug-in the scattering

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:57.370
rate, which is typical coupling
two different particles,

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.380
into the scattering
term, which has

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:03.010
to include all the
possible collisions, all

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.780
the possible scattering.

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:07.360
And of course, we know
this scattering term

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:09.830
is a very complicated.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:13.820
But at the end of
last lecture, I said,

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:20.350
this is very often presented
in books in, say, solid physics

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.720
or electrical electronics.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:29.410
If you split this f into first
or [? zero-order, ?] which are

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:34.000
even term and the odd term,
and using that [? caret ?]

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.240
because any function can always
have even and odd term, right?

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.410
Summation-- any function
is split into even odd term

00:03:41.410 --> 00:03:46.620
summation, and they plug in back
into the scattering integral.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.750
You find out that this
split lead to, actually,

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.980
in the case of example we're
discussing electron phonon led

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:58.980
to two terms in the scattering.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.450
And one of the term
is approximately,

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.830
you can use the
relaxation approximation.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:13.260
And turns out, physically this
term is elastic scattering.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:19.029
It's a process of where there's
no, say, for example, when

00:04:19.029 --> 00:04:21.394
electron phonon scatter,
there's no energy change.

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:25.070
It's just a
direction change, OK?

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.850
Or the other term is an
inelastic scattering.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:33.050
And it turns out, the inelastic
scattering term you cannot,

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:36.290
[INAUDIBLE] speaking, you
cannot use relaxation time

00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:37.800
or approximation.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:40.820
So just don't be
surprised if you

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:43.430
write a paper you use
the Boltzmann equation,

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:47.180
and to that end say people
heard relaxation time say,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.540
I thought that's not valid.

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:51.110
In fact, we had that experience.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.510
That's, say, a [INAUDIBLE].

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:58.070
The Boltzmann equation is not
valid for inelastic scattering

00:04:58.070 --> 00:04:58.800
process.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.240
So this is where
it came from, OK?

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:10.880
And so the point that
I'm going to make last

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.980
is there are different process.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:16.250
You can write similar
expressions [? starting from ?]

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:18.680
along the same approach,
Fermi Golden Rule.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.200
You can calculate
the scatter matrix.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.850
That's the interaction.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:35.320
That's the, say, for example,
if we have semiconductor,

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:39.010
and light comes in, and that
can interact the excited

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:43.150
electron lift the electron from
valence band to the conduction

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:44.210
band.

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:49.720
And we have h new
here, and that process

00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:51.760
you can describe a
using Fermi Golden Rule.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.070
That's the absorption scale--

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:54.620
process.

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.410
So you can calculate
the absorption.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:59.920
Fundamentally, you can calculate
the absorption coefficient

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.010
of material using this approach.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:11.650
And now, once I have the
electron lifted create a hole

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:15.000
and the electron hole in the
valence band and electron

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.490
in the conduction band.

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:20.260
And if you look at
can I get it, right?

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:21.640
Yeah, I want the solar cell.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.230
I want to make a solar cell.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:26.950
And the energy of the
electron hole are here.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:30.010
And of course, you want
to take this energy out

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.080
to the electrode.

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:34.510
Then it's a
competition reprocess

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:38.350
because, if I think
about here, right?

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:41.290
I have electron and have a hole.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:45.550
It turns out there are
different possibilities.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:48.410
Of course, in the
solar cell what happens

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:51.160
you create a gradient
of this charge carrier,

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:54.100
and they will diffuse
towards the electrode.

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:55.900
But that takes time, right?

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:59.620
Any, say, it takes
time for this diffusion

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.190
to happen, and in the
process, the excess energy

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:07.210
of the electron because the
electron phonon scattering--

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:09.100
that's the one that we treated.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:12.910
You can go to, for example,
plug-in typical numbers.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:15.610
And the electron phonon
scattering is very fast.

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:21.220
So it's hard to actually
get the energy out directly,

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.780
and they were relaxed
to the bottom.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:28.060
And if you can extract
this electron energy

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.370
before they relax to
the bottom, there's

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.620
some idea people
tried in the past.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.650
A lot of people reading
this was a hot electron.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:37.510
The electron is still hot.

00:07:37.510 --> 00:07:42.040
And if you can extract them,
those are hot electron cells.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.900
People are reading in the 70s.

00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:48.230
Nobody has really realized
conceptually it's possible.

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:51.800
Nobody has really realized
then the benefit of solar cell.

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:57.580
So similarly, the holes
were relaxed, right?

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:02.000
And once they got here, then
you have the possibility--

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.620
so the electron hole will
recombine and give off

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.240
the energy.

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:11.000
And you can have two
recombination process.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.390
One is the emitter h new.

00:08:13.390 --> 00:08:14.530
This is radiative.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:23.790
So radiative
recombination, right?

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:28.350
And the other possibility
is actually give us

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.420
a lot of phonons, right?

00:08:30.420 --> 00:08:32.850
Again, this is h
[? omega ?] just to say.

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:36.120
It's still h new, but the
[? phonon ?] vibrational energy

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:38.340
is much smaller, right?

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.260
So the band gap energy
is usually larger.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:46.490
So this typically will
involve multi phonon, right,

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.240
multiple phonon processes.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:09:01.680
So this is nonradiative, OK?

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:04.170
So you have
radiative [INAUDIBLE]

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:05.940
radiative recombination.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.950
And of course, if
you say you want

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.560
to make a laser,
for example, you

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.360
want the radiative
recombination.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:14.340
You don't want to lose those.

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:17.730
You want to extract the
energy of the photons, right?

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.910
It turns out that, even you
want to make a solar cell,

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:24.840
as we will talk today, you want
this radiative recombination

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.820
other than non-radiative
recombination.

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.460
But even this is not
all representative

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.660
of all the possibilities.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:40.140
One other possibility is that
now I have a electron here.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:43.680
I have hole here,
and they interact.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.980
Of course, they
release their energy.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:50.720
And in this process, they
don't release two photon.

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:52.940
They don't release two phonon.

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:56.630
They give their energy
to less labor electron.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:58.970
And so what they
happen is this energy

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:05.230
is kick the electron here
to a higher energy state.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:09.650
OK, and again, you know
you don't like this one

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:11.930
because once the
energy-- this electron

00:10:11.930 --> 00:10:16.010
gets to a higher energy state,
you are relaxed quickly back.

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:19.730
So that excess
energy is lost again

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:23.660
to the phonon because
of this process, right?

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:27.600
And unless you can, like I said,
extract the hot electron out.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:39.120
And this is an Auger process,
Auger recombination, OK?

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:43.140
So there are all those
processes, in theory,

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:49.020
you can go to describe using the
quantum mechanical scattering

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:54.150
matrix method and
write it down and, say,

00:10:54.150 --> 00:10:57.970
calculate the scattering rate.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:02.670
So if I write the
Boltzmann equation, say,

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:06.030
so this scattering term is
a lot more complex, right?

00:11:06.030 --> 00:11:13.770
So before we just used the, say,
relaxation time approximation.

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:18.460
And now, you can say I
have a really, for example,

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:23.290
I can have scattering, really,
electron phonon, right?

00:11:23.290 --> 00:11:25.680
This could be electron
phonon scattering.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.470
I could also have,
say, generation, right?

00:11:31.470 --> 00:11:35.880
If I have photon comes in that
will generate electron hole

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:36.930
pairs.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:40.380
Depends on what
carrier, say, consider.

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:46.710
And then I have recombination,
radiative or nonradiative.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.470
All those are possible.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.770
And this was on the right hand
side of the Boltzmann equation,

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:53.970
right?

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:59.450
On the left hand side
is the transport,

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:05.790
is the diffusion, and the moving
under electric field, right?

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:11.400
So at the end, I have a coupling
of the energy conversion.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:15.750
You can think that the right
hand side Boltzmann equation,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:19.420
and the transport is on the
left hand side of the equation.

00:12:19.420 --> 00:12:22.080
And there is the
competition if you

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.570
want to extract the useful
energy out to the electrode.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:35.240
So just in terms of terminology,
a little bit of sidetrack.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:39.680
One terminology, if
you do solar cells,

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:43.220
you do lasers, one
terminology that's used often

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:45.480
is called quantum efficiency.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.390
So what does quantum
efficiency mean?

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:54.590
So it just as
definition, and this

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:57.680
are all engineering
language at the end

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:03.260
because to do those calculations
you can see already it's

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:05.930
very much very involved, right?

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:11.150
So say, the quantum
efficiency depends

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:16.430
on what I'm making, whether
I'm making laser or solar cell,

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:17.120
right?

00:13:17.120 --> 00:13:23.720
So if I'm making, for
example, a laser, what I want,

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:25.920
if I make a laser?

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:30.280
For example, I want
photons, right?

00:13:30.280 --> 00:13:35.860
And I inject a current, which
means I inject a electron hole

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:39.330
pairs, right?

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:43.440
And one pair of electron hole
I inject into the device,

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:45.642
in theory, perfectly.

00:13:45.642 --> 00:13:46.350
What do you want?

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:50.000
You want to get a one
photon out, right?

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.460
So this is the
quantum efficiency

00:13:52.460 --> 00:13:59.750
is the number of photons you get
versus the number of electron

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:06.050
hole you inject, right?

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:12.890
And this is a
laser or, say, LED.

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:19.230
Now, if you use solar cell,
it's a reverse, right?

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.910
You have one photon comes in.

00:14:20.910 --> 00:14:24.880
You want to extract a one
pair of electron holes.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:32.110
And so one electron
pair and one photon.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:37.140
So this is a solar cell, right?

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:44.780
And this is quantum efficiency.

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:47.330
Then you read the
literature, sometimes

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.670
people say what's the
external quantum efficiency?

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:53.210
What's an internal
quantum efficiency, right?

00:14:53.210 --> 00:14:57.182
So you have external.

00:14:57.182 --> 00:15:02.090
You have internal
quantum efficiency.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:08.090
And what to talk about is, say,
for example, you make a device,

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:11.450
make a solar cell, you
have electrode, right?

00:15:11.450 --> 00:15:15.410
What can actually be going
to the electrode that's

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:16.790
externally.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:19.940
But how many electron
hole pairs steady

00:15:19.940 --> 00:15:23.030
state that you
have inside, right,

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:24.560
the device, that's an internal.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:27.980
So you have the difference
between internal and external

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.840
quantum efficiency.

00:15:29.840 --> 00:15:35.960
And people use those to
characterize how effective

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:44.230
because, say, normally we
think one pair of electron hole

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:48.320
will generate one pair
of photon or vice versa.

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:51.590
However, recently, if
you read literature,

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:54.380
there are some people saying
how quantum efficiency larger

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:56.720
than 1.

00:15:56.720 --> 00:15:59.540
What does that mean, right?

00:15:59.540 --> 00:16:04.160
So this is when you think
about that you want to make,

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:10.580
let's suppose, I have
band gap here, right?

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:15.854
And I have very high
energy photons comes in.

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:21.170
And the very high
energy photon--

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:22.540
so this is an electron.

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:23.920
This is a hole.

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.110
And generally, that's
a high energy electron

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.800
in a hole in the band.

00:16:28.800 --> 00:16:33.600
And if you're lucky, these are
all the different rate process.

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:38.160
This electron when
relax down, it

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:41.110
does not interact
with the [? phonon. ?]

00:16:41.110 --> 00:16:46.210
It will actually give
the excess energy

00:16:46.210 --> 00:16:51.640
to lift another electron
out of the valence band

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:55.230
to the conduction band, right?

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:57.930
Of course, that means that
this electron energy has

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.090
to be larger than the band gap.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:02.580
Otherwise, that's
impossible, right?

00:17:02.580 --> 00:17:04.650
And that's multi carrier.

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:08.369
Multiple-- so in this case,
in fact here, the one photon

00:17:08.369 --> 00:17:12.030
